### [REQUIRED] Step 2: Describe your environment

  * Operating System version: Linux (Ubuntu 24.04), OpenJDK 21
  * Firebase SDK version: reproduced on 9.11.0 (latest) and 9.5.0
  * Library version: httpclient5 5.6.4 / httpcore5 5.4.3 (9.11.0 defaults); 9.5.0 with httpcore5 + httpcore5-h2 5.3.6
  * Firebase Product: messaging (affects every API using the default `ApacheHttp2Transport`)

### [REQUIRED] Step 3: Describe the problem

Since 9.4.0 (#979), the default transport is `ApacheHttp2Transport`, which runs network I/O on httpclient5's `httpclient-dispatch-N` threads. If an `Error` (not an `Exception`) is thrown on one of those threads, for example a `LinkageError` from a mismatched transitive dependency, an `OutOfMemoryError`, or a `StackOverflowError`:

1. httpcore5's `IOReactorWorker` rethrows it and the dispatcher thread dies. The error is only printed to stderr by the JVM's default uncaught-exception handler. The SDK never observes it. The `FutureCallback` passed in `ApacheHttp2Request.execute()` is empty, and it isn't invoked in this case anyway.
2. The in-flight request's future is never completed or failed.
3. `ApacheHttp2Request.execute()` calls `responseFuture.get()` with no bound when `readTimeout == 0`. That is the `FirebaseOptions` default: `TimeoutInitializer` always applies the options' timeouts, which overrides google-http-client's 20s defaults. The connect and response timeouts passed to httpclient5 are 0 (disabled), and they would be enforced by the dead dispatcher thread anyway.

Result: `FirebaseMessaging.send()` blocks the calling thread forever, with no exception and no SDK log.

With a non-zero read timeout (9.11.0 only, via #1239), the call fails after the timeout with `Unknown error while making a remote service call: Stream exception in request`, caused by `TimeoutValueException`. The real error isn't in the cause chain, so there is still no way to tell from the SDK what went wrong. Before the HTTP/2 transport, the same `Error` would have been thrown synchronously on the caller's thread.

**Real-world impact.** We hit this in production on 9.5.0. Our build resolved `httpcore5`/`httpcore5-h2` 5.3.6 next to `httpclient5` (firebase-admin only declares `httpclient5`). httpcore5-h2 5.3.4 changed the `ClientH2UpgradeHandler`/`ClientH2PrefaceHandler` constructors that httpclient5 5.3.x/5.4.x call (apache/httpcomponents-core@048bcc1). Each new connection threw `NoSuchMethodError` on the dispatcher thread. The version skew was on our side, and Apache marks those classes `@Internal`. But every thread calling `send()` hung indefinitely, and push delivery silently stopped for about 48 hours with no exception, metric, or SDK log to point at the cause.

#### Steps to reproduce:

`Repro.java` uses only the public API plus the SDK's own `ApacheHttp2Transport.defaultHttpAsyncClientBuilder()`. It sends with a fake token, so a healthy client fails fast with a 401.

```
cs fetch --classpath com.google.firebase:firebase-admin:9.11.0 > cp.txt
java -cp "$(cat cp.txt)" Repro.java                      # control: 401 in ~330ms
java -cp "$(cat cp.txt)" Repro.java inject-error         # hangs (still blocked after 60s)
java -cp "$(cat cp.txt)" Repro.java inject-error 5000    # "Stream exception in request" after 5s, real Error not in cause chain

# Natural trigger on 9.5.0 (no injection): hangs exactly the same way
cs fetch --classpath com.google.firebase:firebase-admin:9.5.0 \
  --force-version org.apache.httpcomponents.core5:httpcore5:5.3.6 \
  --force-version org.apache.httpcomponents.core5:httpcore5-h2:5.3.6 > cp-950.txt
java -cp "$(cat cp-950.txt)" Repro.java
```

Output with `inject-error` on 9.11.0:

```
Exception in thread "httpclient-dispatch-1" java.lang.LinkageError: simulated Error on the I/O dispatch thread
	at Repro$ThrowingListener.connected(Repro.java:70)
	at org.apache.hc.core5.reactor.InternalDataChannel.onTLSSessionStart(InternalDataChannel.java:184)
	...
	at org.apache.hc.core5.reactor.IOReactorWorker.run(IOReactorWorker.java:44)
RESULT: send() still blocked after 60s. fcm-caller stack:
    at org.apache.hc.core5.concurrent.BasicFuture.get(BasicFuture.java:97)
    at com.google.firebase.internal.ApacheHttp2Request.execute(ApacheHttp2Request.java:134)
    at com.google.api.client.http.HttpRequest.execute(HttpRequest.java:1012)
    at com.google.firebase.internal.ErrorHandlingHttpClient.send(ErrorHandlingHttpClient.java:97)
    ...
    at com.google.firebase.messaging.FirebaseMessaging.send(FirebaseMessaging.java:91)
```

On 9.5.0 with httpcore5 5.3.6, the dispatcher dies with `java.lang.NoSuchMethodError: 'void org.apache.hc.core5.http2.impl.nio.ClientH2UpgradeHandler.<init>(org.apache.hc.core5.http2.impl.nio.ClientH2StreamMultiplexerFactory)'` at `HttpAsyncClientProtocolNegotiationStarter.createHandler`. `send()` blocks at `ApacheHttp2Request.java:123`.

#### Relevant Code:

```java
public class Repro {
  public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    FirebaseOptions.Builder options = FirebaseOptions.builder()
        .setProjectId("repro-project")
        .setCredentials(new FakeCredentials());
    if (args.length > 1) {
      options.setReadTimeout(Integer.parseInt(args[1]));
    }
    if (args.length > 0 && args[0].equals("inject-error")) {
      options.setHttpTransport(new ApacheHttp2Transport(
          ApacheHttp2Transport.defaultHttpAsyncClientBuilder()
              .setIOSessionListener(new ThrowingListener())
              .build()));
    }
    FirebaseApp app = FirebaseApp.initializeApp(options.build());

    Future<String> result = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor().submit(() ->
        FirebaseMessaging.getInstance(app).send(Message.builder().setToken("fake-device-token").build()));
    // Healthy client: FirebaseMessagingException (401) in well under a second.
    // Broken dispatcher with default options: never returns.
    System.out.println(result.get(60, TimeUnit.SECONDS));
  }

  static final class FakeCredentials extends GoogleCredentials {
    @Override
    public AccessToken refreshAccessToken() {
      return new AccessToken("fake-token", new Date(System.currentTimeMillis() + 3_600_000));
    }
  }

  static final class ThrowingListener implements IOSessionListener {
    @Override public void connected(IOSession s) { throw new LinkageError("simulated Error on the I/O dispatch thread"); }
    @Override public void startTls(IOSession s) {}
    @Override public void inputReady(IOSession s) {}
    @Override public void outputReady(IOSession s) {}
    @Override public void timeout(IOSession s) {}
    @Override public void exception(IOSession s, Exception ex) {}
    @Override public void disconnected(IOSession s) {}
  }
}
```

#### Suggested fixes

1. **Never wait without a bound in `ApacheHttp2Request.execute()`.** Either ship finite default timeouts in `FirebaseOptions`, or bound the client-side wait even when `readTimeout == 0`. Today the SDK's liveness depends on a background thread it doesn't control.
2. **Surface dispatcher death to callers.** For example, build the default client with `HttpAsyncClientBuilder.setThreadFactory(...)` so dispatcher threads have an `UncaughtExceptionHandler`. That handler can record the `Throwable` and fail outstanding and future requests with an `IOException` whose cause is the `Error`. Alternatively, check `httpAsyncClient.getStatus()` while waiting. httpcore5's I/O reactor exception callback only receives `Exception`s, so it doesn't cover this case.
3. **Log it.** The SDK already depends on SLF4J; logging dispatcher death there would make the root cause visible in applications' normal logs.
4. **Pin `httpcore5` and `httpcore5-h2` explicitly in the POM**, at the version matching the declared `httpclient5`. Dependency resolution in consuming builds is then less likely to pair httpclient5 with an incompatible core. Apache doesn't guarantee compatibility for the `@Internal` classes httpclient5 uses.

#### Workarounds for other users

- Set `FirebaseOptions.Builder#setConnectTimeout` and `setReadTimeout`. On 9.11.0 and later, this turns the hang into a timeout error, though the root cause is still hidden.
- Keep `httpcore5`/`httpcore5-h2` aligned with the `httpclient5` version the SDK declares.

