body_text:
Currently, net/netip.Addr and net/netip.AddrPort perform a string copy when parsing the `text []byte` coming in from UnmarshalText:

https://cs.opensource.google/go/go/+/refs/tags/go1.25.5:src/net/netip/netip.go;l=1010

```go
	*ip, err = ParseAddr(string(text))
```

It seems there is an opportunity to avoid an allocation here. We would like to use `netip.Addr` when unmarshaling some incoming JSON data we know to contain an IP address to avoid the allocation of a `string` type.

Benchmark:

```go
package main

import (
	"net/netip"
	"testing"
)

func BenchmarkNetIPAddrUnmarshalText(b *testing.B) {
	for b.Loop() {
		var addr netip.Addr
		if err := addr.UnmarshalText([]byte("10.1.1.1")); err != nil {
			panic(err)
		}
	}
}
```

```
$ go test -bench=. -benchmem
BenchmarkNetIPAddrUnmarshalText-16      52738260                21.93 ns/op            8 B/op          1 allocs/op
```

