body_text:
### Proposal Details

There is no straightforward way to clone a `hash.XOF` (#69518), while `hash.Cloner` (#69521) exists for `hash.Hash`. In #69521 there had been a suggestion to also add cloning support for `hash.XOF` but this was removed from the final proposal. Both `hash.XOF` and `hash.Cloner` were added in go1.25.

At the moment, the only sanctioned option is to use [encoding.BinaryMarshaler](https://pkg.go.dev/encoding#BinaryMarshaler) and [encoding.BinaryUnmarshaler](https://pkg.go.dev/encoding#BinaryUnmarshaler) to first marshal and then unmarshal the state. This isn't particularly pleasant and lacks the efficiency of what a Clone method could achieve.

I propose we add a `hash.XOFCloner` interface to mirror `hash.Cloner` and extend all `hash.XOF` implementations in the standard library to implement it.

```go
// An XOFCloner is a hash function whose state can be cloned, returning a value
// with equivalent and independent state.
//
// All [XOF] implementations in the standard library implement this interface.
//
// If a hash can only determine at runtime if it can be cloned (e.g. if it wraps
// another hash), Clone may return an error wrapping [errors.ErrUnsupported].
// Otherwise, Clone must always return a nil error.
type XOFCloner interface {
	XOF
	Clone() (XOFCloner, error)
}
```

(I've left references to "a hash (function)" as `hash.XOF` also refers to itself as "a hash function").

If this can't be implemented with the API of existing `crypto/internal/fips140/...` XOFs, then that should be noted in the docs like it is for `hash.Cloner` and an error returned.

`XOFCloner` might not be the nicest name in existence, but I really can't see any better options. It's also sufficiently clear and is distinct, yet related, to `hash.Cloner`. Perhaps it would have been better to add a `Clone` method to `hash.XOF`. Alas that horse has bolted.

---

The only XOF implemented in the standard library is `crypto/sha3`. It does not implement any method called `Clone` so can safely implement this interface.

There are three XOFs implemented in `golang.org/x/crypto`: SHAKE in `x/crypto/sha3` and BLAKE2X in `x/crypto/blake2b` and `x/crypto/blake2s`.

`x/crypto/sha3` is incompatible with the proposed interface, but it has been replaced with `crypto/sha3` and reduced to a wrapper. Users should update their import if they need to rely on `hash.XOFCloner`.

`x/crypto/blake2b` and `x/crypto/blake2s` are also incompatible with the proposed interface. The accepted proposal #65269 will see all packages in `x/crypto` moved into the standard library or deprecated. At that time, they can be updated to have compatible `Clone` methods.

The name `Clone` for the method seems too valuable to me to suggest picking a different name just for those (soon to be legacy) packages. If we really want to enable compatibility, we could define a method like `interface { Cloner() hash.XOFClone }` and allow the `blake2[bs].XOF` interfaces to be upgraded to that. That would provide a standard library compatible cloner as best as we reasonably could. I don't think it's worth the effort though. Instead I'd suggest we just defer compatibility for those until #65269 is resolved.

/cc @golang/security @FiloSottile (as author of related issues)

