## Background

`gcexportdata.Write` encodes a `*types.Package` "deeply": the output contains a complete description of every object reachable from the package's API, including objects defined in dependencies. This is appropriate when the consumer has no other way to obtain dependency type information (the cmd/compile model), but it is wasteful (total export data is worst-case quadratic across a build) for an incremental analysis driver that already type-checks every package in the import graph.

Such a driver wants to encode only the declarations of P itself, representing every reference to a type from package Q as a symbolic `(Q.path, objectpath)` pair to be resolved at decode time against the driver's own copy of Q. This "shallow" form is what gopls has used internally (via `internal/gcimporter.IExportShallow` / `IImportShallow`) since 2023. Exposing it allows analysis drivers built outside this module to use the same approach without vendoring the internal package.

## Proposal

Add three declarations to `golang.org/x/tools/go/gcexportdata`:

```go
// A PackageLookup resolves a package path to a *types.Package,
// typically by a recursive call to [ReadShallow].
type PackageLookup = func(path string) (*types.Package, error)

// WriteShallow encodes "shallow" export data for pkg: only pkg's own
// declarations are encoded; types defined in other packages are
// represented by references to be resolved by [ReadShallow].
//
// Shallow data is smaller and faster to produce than [Write], but
// requires the consumer to supply each referenced package on demand.
// It is intended for incremental analysis drivers.
//
// The encoding is unspecified and may change between versions of this
// module, and is not interchangeable with [Read], [Write], or the
// compiler.
func WriteShallow(out io.Writer, fset *token.FileSet, pkg *types.Package) error

// ReadShallow decodes a package previously encoded by [WriteShallow].
//
// For each distinct package (other than path itself) whose types
// appear in the export data, ReadShallow calls lookup; lookup must
// return the corresponding *types.Package, typically obtained by a
// recursive ReadShallow. All packages in an import graph must share a
// single FileSet, and must share the same logical import graph: if a package
// A imports B and C, and B imports C, the lookup function must return the
// same C that appears in B's imports.
//
//    ReadShallow(A, lookup)
//    ├─ lookup("B") → ReadShallow(B, lookup)
//    │                └─ lookup("C") → c
//    └─ lookup("C") → c   (same c)
//
// The returned package's Imports() reports only packages whose types
// appear in the export data, which may be a subset of, or disjoint
// from, the source file imports.
func ReadShallow(in io.Reader, fset *token.FileSet, path string, lookup PackageLookup) (*types.Package, error)
```

The encoding is prefixed with the format byte `'s'`, distinct from `'i'` (deep indexed, produced by `Write`) and `'u'` (unified, produced by the compiler). `Read` rejects `'s'` data with an error directing the caller to `ReadShallow`. After the format byte the payload is the existing iexport stream, whose leading version varint provides format versioning.

## Discussion

**Why a per-package lookup, not the internal batch callback?** The internal `GetPackagesFunc` exposes the full dependency manifest as a mutable slice with unexported state fields. The per-package form needs no exported types, and gopls (the only current consumer) iterates the batch serially anyway. A batch form can be added later without breaking this API if a driver wants to prefetch dependencies in parallel.

**Why no `name` argument to `PackageLookup`?** The export-data manifest records each dependency's name, but a coherent driver can already map path → package (and thus name) via its own metadata; the name in the manifest is useful only for minting stub packages, which are insufficient under shallow encoding (referenced types must resolve in the dependency's scope). The importer still validates the recorded name against the returned package and reports a mismatch as an error.

**Why `io.Reader`/`io.Writer`?** For symmetry with the existing `Read`/`Write` in this package. Shallow data is always memory-resident in practice; a `*bytes.Reader` fast path in the package's internal `readAll` avoids the extra copy.

**Relationship to #69491.** That proposal would move go/types serialization into the standard library as `types.Export`/`Import`. If accepted, shallow encoding would naturally become an option of `types.ExportOptions` rather than a separate function pair, and these declarations would become thin forwarding wrappers.

**`Imports()` semantics.** As documented on `ReadShallow`, the returned package's `Imports()` lists only packages whose types appear in its export data — not the source file's import set. This is the only thing the export data can report, but it has surprised users of the internal API; documenting it here addresses that.

A prototype is available at [CL 768840](https://go-review.googlesource.com/c/tools/+/768840). As a validation of the API shape, [CL 768841](https://go-review.googlesource.com/c/tools/+/768841) migrates gopls's `importPackage` to `ReadShallow`.

CC @adonovan

