body_text:
### Proposal Details

When using iterators, it is quite common to need loops of the following form:

```go
every := true
for x := range seq {
    if !pred(x) {
        every = false
        break
    }
}
use(every)
```
and its DeMorgan dual:
```go
some := false
for x := range seq {
    if pred(x) {
        some = true
        break
    }
}
use(some)
```

**Proposal:** we propose to add Every and Some as methods of the iter.Seq type:

```go
package iter

// Every reports whether f(x) is true for every element of the sequence.
func (Seq[T]) Every(f func(T) bool) bool

// Some reports whether f(x) is true for some element of the sequence.
// It stops iterating at the first such element.
func (Seq[T]) Some(f func(T) bool) bool
```

along with their counterparts for Seq2:

```go
package iter

// Every reports whether f(k, v) is true for every key/value pair in the sequence.
func (Seq2[K, V]) Every(f func(K, V) bool) bool

// Some reports whether f(k, v) is true for some key/value pair in the sequence.
// It stops iterating at the first such element.
func (Seq2[K, V]) Some(f func(K, V) bool) bool
```

Though the names "all" and "any" are sometimes used for these operators in other languages, those names are  both used for other meanings in Go.

(Aside: it is slightly unfortunate that the implementation of a non-trivial iterator [generally requires](https://pkg.go.dev/golang.org/x/tools/gopls@v0.23.0/internal/analysis/recursiveiter#hdr-Analyzer_recursiveiter) in effect computing Every(seq, yield) internally before discarding the final boolean result. Had the fundamental iteration primitive returned the boolean result--making iteration identical to computing Every(yield)--the extra layer of indirection could have been avoided when the purpose of the loop was to obtain the materialized boolean. Oh well.)

