The data race happens because the request body (the global variable `buf`) is still being read in a separate goroutine after the `insertLogs` function has returned, while at the same time we use the same buffer to send the next request. This behavior is documented in [`http.RoundTripper`](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#RoundTripper):

> RoundTrip must always close the body, including on errors,
> but depending on the implementation may do so in a separate
> goroutine even after RoundTrip returns. This means that
> callers wanting to reuse the body for subsequent requests
> must arrange to wait for the Close call before doing so.

The problem exists for both HTTP/1 and HTTP/2. `(*http.Client).Do` also documents this behavior:

> The request Body, if non-nil, will be closed by the underlying Transport, even on errors. The Body may be closed asynchronously after Do returns.

Note that the sentence `The Body may be closed asynchronously after Do returns.` [was added](https://go-review.googlesource.com/c/go/+/523036) in Go 1.22 and did not exist before that. This means that users who relied on the documentation before that version could only find out about the problem if they looked into how`http.Client` works in more detail. The note that the request body is closed asynchronously is still missing from `http.Post` and `(*http.Client).Post`.

I found the following projects that ran into this behavior:

- https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/issues/108906
- https://github.com/VictoriaMetrics/VictoriaMetrics/issues/11507
- https://github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go/pull/874

My proposal is that `http.Transport` should wait for the request body to be closed at the moment the response body is closed, or when `RoundTrip()` returned an error. This way, in the typical scenario of using `http.Client` - the user sends a request, reads the response, closes the body, then retries the request or reuses the memory - the user will not run into the problem described above. This would significantly reduce the chance of a data race caused by incorrect use of `http.Client` and `http.RoundTripper`.

The downside of this approach is that the `(*Response).Body.Close` method will block the current goroutine:

- For HTTP/1, for [50 milliseconds](https://github.com/golang/go/blob/a14b5433261d7ed98b716b0c71e36a46b7148ef2/src/net/http/transport.go#L2826-L2832), or until the server finishes reading the body.
- For HTTP/2: immediately, if the server responded with a status code [> 299](https://github.com/golang/go/blob/a14b5433261d7ed98b716b0c71e36a46b7148ef2/src/net/http/internal/http2/transport.go#L1167),
  or until the server finishes reading, or until the request timeout fires.

Note that a server is allowed not to read the request body in full, and this is normal behavior. Even so, I consider blocking on close a fair trade-off that improves the user experience of the `http` package and avoids a common mistake made when implementing request retries or when reusing memory (as in the example above).

As an alternative, users can implement a wrapper around `http.Transport` that implements `http.RoundTripper` and arranges the wait when the request body is closed as suggested in the documentation of `RoundTripper`:

