### Go version

go version go1.26.8 linux/amd64. The same code is on go1.27.1 and master: `gcmAesEnc` in `src/crypto/internal/fips140/aes/gcm/gcm_amd64.s` still stores its 8 blocks with `MOVOU Xn, k(SP)`.

### Output of `go env` in your module/workspace:

```shell
GOARCH='amd64'
GOOS='linux'
GOAMD64='v1'
```

CPU: Intel Xeon Gold 6152 (Skylake-SP), KVM guest, benchmark pinned to one vCPU with `taskset`.

### What did you do?

`gcmAesEnc` stores the 8 ciphertext blocks of each 128-byte iteration to its frame with unaligned `MOVOU` at `(SP), 16(SP), ... 112(SP)` and reads them back for GHASH. SP is only guaranteed to be 8-byte aligned. When `SP % 16 == 8` and `SP % 4096` is in `[3976, 4088]`, exactly one of those 16-byte stores crosses a 4 KiB page boundary on every iteration. That is a split store across pages.

Which of the 512 possible 8-byte stack positions a caller lands on depends only on the frame sizes above it. So an unrelated code change can move a TLS server's write path into or out of the slow band. In an HTTP/1.1-over-TLS edge server, one handler's frame grew by 72 bytes, with no change on the write path itself. AES time per encrypted response went up 25-33%, which was 2-3% of total process CPU. We bisected it to that frame change and confirmed it with `go tool objdump` frame sizes.

The repro below sweeps the caller's stack depth in 8-byte steps over more than 4 KiB. At each depth it runs an in-place `Seal` of a 16 KiB record, the TLS 1.3 shape.

```
GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build -o spbench2 . && taskset -c 7 ./spbench2
```

<details><summary>main.go</summary>

```go
// spbench2: in-place AES-GCM Seal of a TLS 1.3-sized record swept over caller stack depth in 8-byte steps,
// printing the caller's stack address mod 4096 against throughput.
package main

import (
	"crypto/aes"
	"crypto/cipher"
	"fmt"
	"runtime/debug"
	"time"
	"unsafe"
)

var (
	gcm   cipher.AEAD
	nonce = make([]byte, 12)
	out   = make([]byte, 18432)
	sink  uintptr
)

//go:noinline
func seal(iters int) (uintptr, float64) {
	var mark uint64
	best := 0.0
	for r := 0; r < 3; r++ {
		t0 := time.Now()
		for i := 0; i < iters; i++ {
			rec := out[:5+16384]
			gcm.Seal(rec[:5], nonce, rec[5:], rec[:5])
		}
		if v := float64(iters) * 16384 / time.Since(t0).Seconds() / 1e6; v > best {
			best = v
		}
	}
	return uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&mark)), best
}

//go:noinline
func pad1(iters int) (uintptr, float64) {
	var p [1]uint64
	sink += uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&p[0]))
	return seal(iters)
}

//go:noinline
func pad2(iters int) (uintptr, float64) {
	var p [2]uint64
	sink += uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&p[0]))
	return seal(iters)
}

//go:noinline
func pad3(iters int) (uintptr, float64) {
	var p [3]uint64
	sink += uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&p[0]))
	return seal(iters)
}

//go:noinline
func pad4(iters int) (uintptr, float64) {
	var p [4]uint64
	sink += uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&p[0]))
	return seal(iters)
}

//go:noinline
func pad5(iters int) (uintptr, float64) {
	var p [5]uint64
	sink += uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&p[0]))
	return seal(iters)
}

//go:noinline
func pad6(iters int) (uintptr, float64) {
	var p [6]uint64
	sink += uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&p[0]))
	return seal(iters)
}

//go:noinline
func pad7(iters int) (uintptr, float64) {
	var p [7]uint64
	sink += uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&p[0]))
	return seal(iters)
}

//go:noinline
func pad8(iters int) (uintptr, float64) {
	var p [8]uint64
	sink += uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&p[0]))
	return seal(iters)
}

var pads = []func(int) (uintptr, float64){pad1, pad2, pad3, pad4, pad5, pad6, pad7, pad8}

//go:noinline
func deeper(n, variant, iters int) (uintptr, float64) {
	var p [3]uint64
	sink += uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(&p[0]))
	if n > 0 {
		return deeper(n-1, variant, iters)
	}
	return pads[variant](iters)
}

func main() {
	debug.SetGCPercent(-1)
	b, _ := aes.NewCipher(make([]byte, 16))
	gcm, _ = cipher.NewGCM(b)
	done := make(chan struct{})
	go func() {
		deeper(2000, 0, 1)
		for n := 0; n < 140; n++ {
			for v := 0; v < 8; v++ {
				sp, mbs := deeper(n, v, 150)
				fmt.Printf("n=%d v=%d spmod=%d spmod16=%d MB/s=%.0f\n", n, v, sp%4096, sp%16, mbs)
			}
		}
		close(done)
	}()
	<-done
}
```

</details>

### What did you see happen?

The median over all 512 positions is 4033 MB/s. The 8 predicted page-split positions run at 2680-3190 MB/s, 21-33% slower. The same 8 positions are slow again 4096 bytes deeper. `gcmAesEnc` SP was computed from the caller's `&mark` using the `go tool objdump` frame sizes of this binary: `main.seal` 0x70 to `&mark`, `(*GCM).Seal` 0x68, `sealAfterIndicator` 0x98, `seal` 0xa0 and `gcmAesEnc` 0x100, each plus 16 for the return address and saved BP.

```
median over all 512 positions: 4033 MB/s
rows shown: every predicted page-split position, every position below 85% of median, and neighbours

caller &mark %4096 | gcmAesEnc SP %4096 | SP%16 | MB/s (2 samples, 4096 B apart) | page split
              272 |               3520 |     0 |                     3231, 4158 | no
              696 |               3944 |     8 |                     3796, 4280 | no
              712 |               3960 |     8 |                     3871, 4010 | no
              728 |               3976 |     8 |                     2932, 3158 | YES
              744 |               3992 |     8 |                     3025, 3056 | YES
              760 |               4008 |     8 |                     2960, 2931 | YES
              776 |               4024 |     8 |                     3185, 3055 | YES
              792 |               4040 |     8 |                     2965, 3028 | YES
              808 |               4056 |     8 |                     2874, 2844 | YES
              824 |               4072 |     8 |                     2689, 2681 | YES
              840 |               4088 |     8 |                     2682, 2725 | YES
              856 |                  8 |     8 |                     3925, 4148 | no
              872 |                 24 |     8 |                     3899, 4191 | no
             1808 |                960 |     0 |                     3916, 3071 | no
             2016 |               1168 |     0 |                     4048, 3337 | no
             3712 |               2864 |     0 |               2205, 4056, 4113 | no
```

A few positions far from the band show single noisy samples, such as 2205 at 2864; their second sample is normal. Only the band is slow in both samples.

Ruled out on the production binary: `GODEBUG=cpu.avx512f=off` and `cpu.avx2=off` (dirty upper state), and `asyncpreemptoff=1`. None of them changed the slowdown.

### What did you expect to see?

AES-GCM throughput that does not depend on the caller's stack depth.

A possible fix is to keep the scratch area 16-byte aligned. For example, point a register at an aligned scratch base (`LEAQ` plus `ANDQ $~15`) inside a frame over-allocated by 16, and address the blocks from that register instead of from SP. `gcmAesDec` and the other SP-relative scratch stores in this file may be worth checking for the same pattern.

