body_text:
### Proposal Details

Follow #46465.

io.LimitedReader lets ReadFrom recognize a byte limit on the source side,
enabling zero-copy (splice / sendfile / copy_file_range) bounded by N.
There is no equivalent on the write side.

On WriteTo paths (net.TCPConn.WriteTo, *os.File.WriteTo, third-party
WriterTo implementations) the caller often cannot attach a limit to the
source, because the source is an unexported field of the type that
implements WriteTo:

    // go-proxyproto
    type Conn struct {
        conn net.Conn // unexported
        ...
    }

    func (p *Conn) WriteTo(w io.Writer) (int64, error) {
        ...
        return io.Copy(w, p.conn)
    }

The caller only controls w. There is no standard way to say "write at
most N bytes" on the destination, so WriteTo either falls back to a
user-space copy or risks writing past the boundary.

Add io.LimitedWriter, mirroring io.LimitedReader:

    package io

    type LimitedWriter struct {
        W Writer
        N int64
    }

    func (l *LimitedWriter) Write(p []byte) (int, error) {
        if l.N <= 0 {
            return 0, EOF
        }
        if int64(len(p)) > l.N {
            p = p[:l.N]
        }
        n, err := l.W.Write(p)
        l.N -= int64(n)
        return n, err
    }

WriteTo implementations may recognize *io.LimitedWriter and honor N in
one kernel call:

    func (c *TCPConn) WriteTo(w io.Writer) (int64, error) {
        if lw, ok := w.(*io.LimitedWriter); ok {
            n, err := c.writeToLimited(lw.W, lw.N)
            lw.N -= n
            return n, err
        }
        return c.writeToFallback(w)
    }

This mirrors ReadFrom's handling of *io.LimitedReader.

Also, the standard library's fallback helpers needs forward the limit:

    func genericWriteTo(c *TCPConn, w io.Writer) (int64, error) {
        var r io.Reader = tcpConnWithoutWriteTo{TCPConn: c}
        lw, ok := w.(*io.LimitedWriter)
        if !ok {
            return io.Copy(w, r)
        }
        lr := &io.LimitedReader{R: r, N: lw.N}
        n, err := io.Copy(lw.W, lr)
        lw.N = lr.N
        return n, err
    }

    func genericReadFrom(f *File, r io.Reader) (int64, error) {
        var w io.Writer = onlyWriter{f}
        lr, ok := r.(*io.LimitedReader)
        if !ok {
            return io.Copy(w, r)
        }
        lw := &io.LimitedWriter{W: w, N: lr.N}
        n, err := io.Copy(lw, &readerOnly{lr.R})
        lr.N = lw.N
        return n, err
    }

Unwrapping is required so the inner os.File.ReadFrom / net.TCPConn.WriteTo
sees the bare opposite type and can still hit its fast path; moving the
limit to the opposite side is required so that fast path knows N.

Why not just LimitedReader? LimitedReader limits the source. WriteTo often
writes from a source the caller cannot see (see go-proxyproto), so the
limit must be expressible on the destination side.

