### govulncheck version

Go: go1.27.1 linux/amd64
Scanner: govulncheck@v1.2.0
DB: https://vuln.go.dev
DB updated: 2026-10-01 20:24:15 +0000 UTC

### Does this issue reproduce at the latest version of golang.org/x/vuln?

Yes

### What did you do?

Ran `govulncheck` in SARIF mode and uploaded the resulting SARIF file using `github/codeql-action/upload-sarif`:

```bash
govulncheck -format sarif ./... > results.sarif
```

Workflow step:
```yaml
- uses: golang/govulncheck-action@v1
  with:
    output-format: 'sarif'
    output-file: 'results.sarif'
- uses: github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v4
  with:
    sarif_file: 'results.sarif'
```

Repository reproducing the issue: `github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/prometheus-engine`

### What did you see happen?

`github/codeql-action/upload-sarif` failed schema validation and rejected the file with:

```text
##[error]Unable to upload "results.sarif" as it is not valid SARIF:
- instance.runs[0].results[1].stacks contains duplicate item
```

Detailed error from schema validator:
```json
{
  "property": "instance.runs[0].results[1].stacks",
  "message": "contains duplicate item",
  "name": "uniqueItems",
  "stack": "instance.runs[0].results[1].stacks contains duplicate item"
}
```

In the generated `results.sarif` for finding `GO-2026-6505` (`go.opentelemetry.io/otel/sdk`), `results[1].stacks` contained 63 call stacks, where Stack 45 and Stack 46 are byte-for-byte duplicates:

```json
{
  "message": {
    "text": "A call stack for vulnerable function go.opentelemetry.io/otel/sdk/trace.logDropped"
  },
  "frames": [
    {
      "module": "github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/prometheus-engine@",
      "location": {
        "physicalLocation": {
          "artifactLocation": {
            "uri": "pkg/operator/operator.go",
            "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
          },
          "region": {
            "startLine": 344,
            "startColumn": 86
          }
        },
        "message": {
          "text": "github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/prometheus-engine/pkg/operator.*Operator.Run"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "module": "sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime@v0.23.3",
      "location": {
        "physicalLocation": {
          "artifactLocation": {
            "uri": "sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime@v0.23.3/pkg/manager/internal.go",
            "uriBaseId": "%GOMODCACHE%"
          },
          "region": {
            "startLine": 280,
            "startColumn": 25
          }
        },
        "message": {
          "text": "sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime/pkg/manager.*controllerManager.GetWebhookServer"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "module": "stdlib@v1.27.1",
      "location": {
        "physicalLocation": {
          "artifactLocation": {
            "uri": "src/sync/once.go",
            "uriBaseId": "%GOROOT%"
          },
          "region": {
            "startLine": 69,
            "startColumn": 11
          }
        },
        "message": {
          "text": "sync.*Once.Do"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "module": "stdlib@v1.27.1",
      "location": {
        "physicalLocation": {
          "artifactLocation": {
            "uri": "src/sync/once.go",
            "uriBaseId": "%GOROOT%"
          },
          "region": {
            "startLine": 78,
            "startColumn": 4
          }
        },
        "message": {
          "text": "sync.*Once.doSlow"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "module": "go.opentelemetry.io/otel/sdk@v1.44.0",
      "location": {
        "physicalLocation": {
          "artifactLocation": {
            "uri": "go.opentelemetry.io/otel/sdk@v1.44.0/trace/evictedqueue.go",
            "uriBaseId": "%GOMODCACHE%"
          },
          "region": {
            "startLine": 58,
            "startColumn": 23
          }
        },
        "message": {
          "text": "go.opentelemetry.io/otel/sdk/trace.logDropped"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

### What did you expect to see?

`govulncheck -format=sarif` should produce valid SARIF 2.1.0 conforming to the OASIS specification.

According to SARIF v2.1.0 §3.27.18 (`result.stacks`):
```json
"stacks": {
  "description": "An array of 'stack' objects relevant to the result.",
  "type": "array",
  "minItems": 0,
  "uniqueItems": true,
  "default": [],
  "items": {
    "$ref": "#/definitions/stack"
  }
}
```
All items in `stacks` must be unique.

### Root Cause Analysis

In [`golang/vuln` `internal/sarif/handler.go`](https://github.com/golang/vuln/blob/master/internal/sarif/handler.go#L308-L322):
```go
func stacks(h *handler, fs []*govulncheck.Finding) []Stack {
	if fs[0].Trace[0].Function == "" { // not call level findings
		return nil
	}

	var stacks []Stack
	for _, f := range fs {
		stacks = append(stacks, stack(h, f))
	}
	// Sort stacks for deterministic output. We sort by message
	// which is effectively sorting by full symbol name. The
	// performance should not be an issue here.
	sort.SliceStable(stacks, func(i, j int) bool { return stacks[i].Message.Text < stacks[j].Message.Text })
	return stacks
}
```

`stacks()` maps each finding `f` to a `Stack` and appends it without deduplication.

When analyzing under Go 1.27.1, generic type arguments are omitted or simplified in the symbol names (e.g. `logDropped[Event]` vs `logDropped[Link]` both become `go.opentelemetry.io/otel/sdk/trace.logDropped`). Two distinct call paths originating from the same call site collapse into identical frame locations and messages, resulting in identical `Stack` objects being appended to the array.

`stacks()` should deduplicate identical `Stack` entries before returning, similar to how duplicate tags were addressed in #75890.

