body_text:
There is a recurring need in analysis tools to know whether a given package is transitively error-free, i.e. whether it corresponds to source that would successfully compile. Currently there is no easy way to compute this predicate from the go/types public API: it requires computing a fold across the package import graph of whether the types.Config.Error handler was called OR the inputs to that package had list/read/parse errors. Yet the computation of types is itself a fold across all inputs, so the machinery should be helping us.

We propose the following API changes:

```go
package types

type Config struct {
...
     HasErrors bool // the current package has metadata/read/parse errors
}

// Compiles reports whether the current package comes from source
// that is transitively error-free, and thus would compile.
// It is the conjunction of four terms:
// - !Config.HasErrors, meaning this package did not have parse (etc) errors, AND
// - the Config.Error handler was not (or would not) have been called,
//   meaning this package has no type errors of its own; AND
// - each dependency in [Package.Imports] also [Package.Compiles],
//   meaning all transitive dependencies would compile too; AND
// - the value of the most recent SetCompile call for this package, if any,
//   meaning it was deserialized from export data of a package that compiles.
func (*Package) Compiles()  bool

// SetCompiles(false) causes Compiles to return false.
// It is provides for use by the type export/import machinery
// to serialize the Package.Compiles flag.
// It should be used only as part of the construction
// of a deserialized package.
func (*Package) SetCompiles(bool)
```

Naming suggestions welcome.

@griesemer @mrkfrmn

