### What did you do?

Benchmarked html.Parse and the Tokenizer across x/net versions on an attribute-heavy page (a product grid: 500 items, about 31 attributes per item, all lower case, no duplicates, no NUL bytes)

```package repro

import (
	"bytes"
	"fmt"
	"strings"
	"testing"

	"golang.org/x/net/html"
)

func page() []byte {
	var b strings.Builder
	b.WriteString(`<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Products</title></head><body><ul class="grid" role="list">`)
	for i := 0; i < 500; i++ {
		fmt.Fprintf(&b, `<li class="item item--card" id="p%d" data-id="%d" data-sku="SKU-%06d" data-category="shoes" data-position="%d" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Product">`, i, i, i, i)
		fmt.Fprintf(&b, `<a class="item__link" href="/p/%d" title="Product %d" data-track="click" rel="nofollow" aria-label="Product %d">`, i, i, i)
		fmt.Fprintf(&b, `<img class="item__img" src="/img/%d.jpg" alt="Product %d" width="300" height="300" loading="lazy" decoding="async">`, i, i)
		fmt.Fprintf(&b, `<span class="item__name" itemprop="name">Product %d</span>`, i)
		fmt.Fprintf(&b, `<span class="item__price" itemprop="price" content="%d.99" data-currency="USD">$%d.99</span>`, i, i)
		b.WriteString(`</a><button type="button" class="btn btn--add" data-action="add-to-cart" aria-label="Add to cart">Add</button></li>`)
	}
	b.WriteString(`</ul></body></html>`)
	return []byte(b.String())
}

var input = page()

func BenchmarkParse(b *testing.B) {
	b.SetBytes(int64(len(input)))
	b.ReportAllocs()
	for b.Loop() {
		if _, err := html.Parse(bytes.NewReader(input)); err != nil {
			b.Fatal(err)
		}
	}
}

// Only Next: never calls TagName or TagAttr.
func BenchmarkTokenizerNext(b *testing.B) {
	b.SetBytes(int64(len(input)))
	b.ReportAllocs()
	for b.Loop() {
		z := html.NewTokenizer(bytes.NewReader(input))
		for z.Next() != html.ErrorToken {
		}
	}
}

func BenchmarkTokenizerTagAttr(b *testing.B) {
	b.SetBytes(int64(len(input)))
	b.ReportAllocs()
	for b.Loop() {
		z := html.NewTokenizer(bytes.NewReader(input))
		for {
			tt := z.Next()
			if tt == html.ErrorToken {
				break
			}
			if tt == html.StartTagToken || tt == html.SelfClosingTagToken {
				_, more := z.TagName()
				for more {
					_, _, more = z.TagAttr()
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
```

### What did you see happen?

Tokenizer.Next alone went from 8 allocations per page to about 2 per attribute (31,018 on this page), even when the caller never reads an attribute:

| version | Next allocs/op | what changed in `readTag` |
|---|---|---|
| v0.54.0 | 8 | nothing |
| v0.55.0 | 15,514 | [CL 781685](https://go.dev/cl/781685) (CVE-2026-27136, [#79575](https://go.dev/issue/79575)): `key := strings.ToLower(string(...))` and `z.attrNames[key]` for every attribute |
| v0.56.0 | 31,018 | [CL 782260](https://go.dev/cl/782260): `key := string(lower(bytes.Clone(...)))`, two allocations per attribute |

The key is also built when `saveAttr` is false and when the key is empty, so
end tags with attributes pay it too.

[#82128](https://go.dev/issue/82128) (Tokenizer.TagName and TagAttr allocate for every tag since v0.56.0) and [CL 848445](https://go.dev/cl/848445) fix the copies in `TagName` and `TagAttr` added by [CL 781883](https://go.dev/cl/781883). They do not touch this path: with [CL 848445](https://go.dev/cl/848445) applied, `Next` and `Parse` still allocate for every attribute.

