### What did you expect to see?

Duplicate detection should not allocate when a tag has few attributes, which is nearly always the case, and should stay linear on any input.


### Possible fix (on top of [CL 848445](https://go.dev/cl/848445))

1. **Duplicate check in `readTag`** (this issue). A new `attrSeen` method hashes the lower-cased key with `hash/maphash` (random seed, key copied to a 64-byte stack array, no allocation) and compares it with the hashes of the keys already saved in the tag, kept in a `[32]uint64` array in the  `Tokenizer`. Two keys are compared byte by byte only when their hashes are equal. A tag with more than 32 saved attributes, or a key longer than 64 bytes, switches the rest of the tag to `z.attrNames`, as today. Each attribute therefore costs one hash, at most 32 integer comparisons and (barring a 64-bit collision) at most one key comparison, so the input stays linear and the quadratic case fixed by [CL 781685](https://go.dev/cl/781685) does not come back. The map is created only when a tag needs it.
2. **`TagName` and `TagAttr`** (for [CL 848445](https://go.dev/cl/848445)). On this page `replaceNUL` takes about 10% of `TagAttr` time: three `bytes.Contains` calls per attribute on short slices. `lowerReplaceNUL` looks for NUL and upper-case letters in the same pass, and `attrVal` returns the value as is unless it holds a NUL, a CR or an `&`, in which case it runs `unescape(convertNewlines(replaceNUL(v)), true)` as today. The output and the in-place lower-casing of the buffer are the same.

