body_text:
### Proposal Details

As per section 2.2 of RFC 5480 regarding Elliptic Curve Cryptography:

> Implementations of Elliptic Curve Cryptography according to this document MUST support the uncompressed form and MAY support the compressed form of the ECC public key.

Go choose to support only the uncompressed form of the EC public key. The idea is to add the `ParseCompressedPublicKey` function (as opposed to `ParseUncompressedPublicKey`) in the `crypto/ecdsa` package:

```go
// ParseCompressedPublicKey parses a public key encoded as a compressed
// point according to SEC 1, Version 2.0, Section 2.3.3 (also known as the X9.62
// compressed format). It returns an error if the point is not in compressed
// form, is not on the curve, or is the point at infinity.
//
// curve must be one of [elliptic.P224], [elliptic.P256], [elliptic.P384], or
// [elliptic.P521], or ParseCompressedPublicKey returns an error.
//
// ParseCompressedPublicKey accepts the same format as
// [ecdh.Curve.NewPublicKey] does for NIST curves, but returns a [PublicKey]
// instead of an [ecdh.PublicKey].
//
// Note that public keys are more commonly encoded in DER (or PEM) format, which
// can be parsed with [crypto/x509.ParsePKIXPublicKey] (and [encoding/pem]).
func ParseCompressedPublicKey(curve elliptic.Curve, data []byte) (*PublicKey, error)
```

Which can then be used in `crypto/x509.ParseCertificate` to allows to parse compressed EC public key in certificates.

I've already made a CL https://go-review.googlesource.com/c/go/+/849047 regarding these changes.

